\begin{abstract}
Small open language models can extract numbers from SEC filings with evidence a machine can check, but some of their wrong answers still pass the check. We ask which numbers cause those errors. In a pre-registered follow-up to Filing-Evidence \citep{nandula2026filingevidence}, we built << d.n_questions|thou >> questions over << d.n_filings >> recent 10-K and 10-Q filings of << d.n_companies >> US companies in ten sectors, with gold answers from the filers' inline XBRL and difficulty tags on every question, all fixed before any model ran. Eleven instruct models (7--21B parameters) answered on one RTX~4090 in three setups: plain, check-and-retry, and thinking. Sector explains little: pooled accuracy ranges from << H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc|pct >> to << H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc|pct >> across sectors, against << H.acc_bottom|pct >> to << H.acc_top|pct >> across models, and the sector term is not significant ($p = << d.h1.p|p >>$). Presentation explains much more. A unit stated away from the figure raises the odds of a wrong answer that passes the check << hh.H2.or|num >>-fold. Each additional figure on the line, a later column and a year-to-date period each lower the odds of a correct answer (odds ratios << hh.H3.or|num(2) >>, << hh.H4.or|num(2) >>, << hh.H5.or|num(2) >>). The sector with the most such errors, oil and gas, is explained by numbers whose unit appears nowhere on the page; << (100*H.og_digits)|num(0) >>\% of those errors are the right digits at the wrong scale. Thinking reduces such errors for << H.c3_lower|length >> of << H.c3_models|length >> models; retrying after a failed check raises them for << H.c2_raises|length >> of 10. The findings hold under a second prompt and a repeat run. Rendering tables as Markdown reduces wrong-column errors but shows how fragile a strict quote check is. We release the questions, all << d.answers_total|thou >> answers, the code and the protocol.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} language models, evaluation, SEC filings, XBRL, information extraction, verifiable evidence, pre-registration

\section{Introduction}
Filing-Evidence \citep{nandula2026filingevidence} asked whether small open models can read SEC filings with evidence a machine can check: the page and a verbatim quote containing the number. They largely can; the best model answered 82.7\% of questions correctly with a verifiable quote. But a share of answers passed every automatic check and were still wrong, with the right line and the wrong number. These \emph{confident-wrong} answers are what make a model unsafe to trust.

Filing-Evidence suggested where such errors come from (units printed in a header rather than beside the number, and neighbouring columns) but, with 1,000 questions, could not test it. This study does. We wrote hypotheses first, built << d.n_questions|thou >> new questions across ten sectors with difficulty tags computed by code, froze everything, and only then ran the models. Three research questions were registered in advance:
\begin{description}
\item[RQ1.] How does accuracy with checkable evidence vary across ten industry sectors?
\item[RQ2.] Which properties of a number make it hard: where its units are stated, how many figures share its line, which column it sits in, its period, and whether it is written in words?
\item[RQ3.] Do the earlier findings on check-and-retry and thinking hold on a larger and more varied set?
\end{description}
The answer to RQ1 is mostly no, and RQ2 explains why: what makes a number hard is how it is printed, and that varies more within every sector than between them.

\section{Related work}
Financial question answering has been studied with FinQA \citep{chen2021finqa}, TAT-QA \citep{zhu2021tatqa}, FinanceBench \citep{islam2023financebench} and DocFinQA \citep{reddy2024docfinqa}, which focus mainly on answer correctness and numerical reasoning. Work on attribution evaluates whether generated statements are supported by cited sources \citep{bohnet2022attributed,gao2023alce,liu2023verifiability}. Filing-Evidence \citep{nandula2026filingevidence} combined the two in a fully automatic setting, with gold answers from inline XBRL \citep{xbrl2013inline}. This study keeps that method and asks which properties of the data predict failure, testing pre-registered hypotheses across industries.

\section{Design}
\begin{figure}[H]
\centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{timeline.pdf}
\caption{The study in time. The protocol and the frozen questions were committed publicly before any model ran; the robustness checks were fully specified before any of them ran.}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{Companies and filings.} Ten sectors of ten companies each, chosen because their statements are laid out differently: banks, insurance, real estate, utilities, oil and gas, metals and mining, healthcare, technology, media and telecom. For each company we took its most recent 10-K and 10-Q from SEC EDGAR \citep{sec_edgar}: << d.n_filings >> filings. No company or datapoint from Filing-Evidence is reused.
\paragraph{Questions and answer key.} Gold answers come from the filers' inline XBRL tags. Each question shows three pages: the answer page, a neighbouring page and a distractor. There are 1,000 questions per sector; << d.n_answerable|thou >> have an answer on the pages shown, and the rest test whether the model answers ``not found''. Page rendering, wording rules, prompt and checker are those of Filing-Evidence, unchanged.
\paragraph{Difficulty tags.} Computed by code before any run: where the unit is stated (row label, the line itself, a table or column header, a page header, or nowhere on the pages shown); the number of figures on the evidence line; whether the answer is the first figure or a later one; the period length; and whether the amount uses a word scale (``\$12.6 billion'').
\paragraph{Outcomes.} \emph{Correct}: right value, and the quote passes every check. \emph{Confident-wrong}: wrong value, but the quote passes every check. \emph{Caught-wrong}: wrong value, and a check fails. \emph{Unverifiable-right}: right value, but the quote is unusable. \emph{Missed}: the model says ``not found'' although the number is there. For numbers that are absent, the outcome is \emph{abstained correctly} or \emph{invented}.

\section{Models and setups}
The ten models of Filing-Evidence at the same pinned revisions, plus Qwen3-8B as a supplementary model, served with vLLM~0.33 \citep{kwon2023vllm} on one RTX~4090 capped at 450\,W, temperature~0. Plain (C1) and check-and-retry (C2) run on all 10{,}000 questions. Thinking (C3) runs on a stratified 1{,}000-question subset for the six models with a reasoning mode, with up to 8{,}192 new tokens. Statistics: 95\% Wilson intervals \citep{wilson1927}; paired exact McNemar tests \citep{mcnemar1947}; Holm correction \citep{holm1979}; Kendall's $\tau$ \citep{kendall1938}. In total << d.answers_total|thou >> answers.

