\section{Robustness}
Three checks, specified in full before any of them ran, all in the plain setup on the 1{,}000-question subset (Table~\ref{tab:robust}).
\paragraph{R3: repeat run.} The identical configuration run again changes the outcome of << H.r3_min|pct >>--<< H.r3_max|pct >> of questions for the three models tested, with no significant change in accuracy or confident-wrong.
\paragraph{R2: a different prompt.} New wording and a different worked example leave the model ranking almost unchanged (Kendall's $\tau = << H.tau_r2|num(2) >>$). The two weakest models, Nemotron-Nano-9B-v2 and OLMo-3-7B, lose accuracy and gain confident-wrong answers under the new wording.
\paragraph{R1: Markdown tables (H8).} Tables rendered as Markdown, one row per line with column headers first. Wrong-period-or-column errors fall from << H.h8.c1 >> to << H.h8.r1 >> (pooled exact McNemar, $p = << H.h8.p|p >>$): \textbf{H8 is supported}, and confident-wrong falls for every model. But strict accuracy collapses for most models, because many quote the row without its pipes and the pre-registered check, applied to the page text exactly as shown, rejects the quote; the ranking scrambles ($\tau = << H.tau_r1|num(2) >>$). Grading R1 a second time with the pipes ignored, a decision made after seeing these results and therefore exploratory, removes most of the collapse and restores the ranking ($\tau = << H.tau_pf|num(2) >>$). The best models read Markdown tables as well as flat text; the others lose up to << H.r1_pf_max_loss|num(0) >> points. The tag effects of H2--H4 keep their direction under R1 and R2.
